1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,240
When health runs low in DOOM,

2
00:00:01,240 --> 00:00:03,840
getting closer to an enemy can be the answer.

3
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:05,760
Rather than encouraging hiding and waiting,

4
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:08,680
the developers wanted reasons to engage in combat.

5
00:00:08,680 --> 00:00:12,340
Follow the loop from approach and attack to recovery.

6
00:00:12,340 --> 00:00:14,660
Waiting behind cover for health

7
00:00:14,660 --> 00:00:16,440
would interrupt the flow of combat.

8
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:18,760
The designers placed recovery opportunities near enemies

9
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:20,920
to encourage re-entering the fight.

10
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:22,400
Attacking can make an enemy stagger,

11
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:24,140
opening a finishing opportunity.

12
00:00:24,140 --> 00:00:25,740
This state is called stagger.

13
00:00:25,740 --> 00:00:27,160
It does not last forever.

14
00:00:27,160 --> 00:00:30,700
Miss the opportunity and the enemy may resume fighting.

15
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:33,060
You have a moment to decide whether to close in.

16
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:35,580
The enemy briefly flashes and staggers,

17
00:00:35,580 --> 00:00:38,060
creating a short opening to approach.

18
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:39,060
Wait too long

19
00:00:39,060 --> 00:00:41,080
and that opportunity can disappear.

20
00:00:41,080 --> 00:00:42,880
Finish at close range with a Glory Kill

21
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:44,460
and receive resource rewards.

22
00:00:44,460 --> 00:00:46,240
This provides more than a tool for attacking:

23
00:00:46,240 --> 00:00:49,580
it gives a reason to take the risk of approaching.

24
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:52,540
Getting needed recovery when health is low

25
00:00:52,540 --> 00:00:53,960
helps encourage forward movement.

26
00:00:53,960 --> 00:00:56,680
Watch the blue health drops after the finisher

27
00:00:56,680 --> 00:00:58,520
and the health increase that follows.

28
00:00:58,520 --> 00:00:59,640
Reducing the enemy count

29
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:02,480
also supplies resources for the next exchange.

30
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:04,040
The presentation also explains how low health

31
00:01:04,040 --> 00:01:07,160
can affect the recovery reward.

32
00:01:07,160 --> 00:01:11,000
The rules for granting health and ammunition are designed separately.

33
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,180
Design each resource type and reward amount separately.

34
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:17,200
This helps avoid a solution that repeats only one action.

35
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:19,080
A healthy player and a wounded player

36
00:01:19,080 --> 00:01:21,560
do not need exactly the same reward.

37
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:22,980
Separate the rules that help restore health

38
00:01:22,980 --> 00:01:25,600
from those that provide ammunition.

39
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:26,660
When ammunition runs short,

40
00:01:26,660 --> 00:01:29,500
the chainsaw provides a large resource reward.

41
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:31,080
It costs fuel in return.

42
00:01:31,080 --> 00:01:33,480
Even actions that obtain resources have a cost.

43
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:36,960
The chainsaw’s ammunition reward requires fuel.

44
00:01:36,960 --> 00:01:38,060
When ammunition runs low,

45
00:01:38,060 --> 00:01:41,180
the chainsaw and its remaining fuel become relevant.

46
00:01:41,180 --> 00:01:43,480
It is a resource trade-off,

47
00:01:43,480 --> 00:01:45,680
rather than a free recovery button.

48
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:46,760
Create a stagger opportunity,

49
00:01:46,760 --> 00:01:48,000
close in for a finish,

50
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:50,020
and use the rewards to keep fighting.

51
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:53,400
DOOM creates a path to recovery during combat.

52
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:55,160
To survive, move forward:

53
00:01:55,160 --> 00:01:56,800
the rewards support that behavior.

54
00:01:56,800 --> 00:01:58,920
The reward for approaching danger

55
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:01,080
creates room for the next attack.
